1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,900
When the universe was very young,

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:05,740
it made matter and antimatter
in almost exactly equal amounts.

3
00:00:06,217 --> 00:00:09,357
And when matter meets antimatter,
both of them disappear.

4
00:00:10,157 --> 00:00:12,337
All that's left is a flash of light.

5
00:00:13,057 --> 00:00:15,417
So by now, the universe should be empty.

6
00:00:16,317 --> 00:00:18,537
No stars, no planets, no people.

7
00:00:19,157 --> 00:00:21,797
Just a faint glow of light,
spreading out forever.

8
00:00:22,565 --> 00:00:24,085
But it isn't empty.

9
00:00:24,385 --> 00:00:27,005
Because the two amounts
weren't quite equal.

10
00:00:27,676 --> 00:00:30,236
For every billion particles of antimatter,

11
00:00:30,316 --> 00:00:33,236
there were about a billion and one
particles of matter.

12
00:00:33,937 --> 00:00:36,577
The billions met and destroyed each other.

13
00:00:37,197 --> 00:00:39,257
The one in a billion was left over.

14
00:00:39,377 --> 00:00:42,377
And everything you have ever seen
is made of that leftover.

15
00:00:43,509 --> 00:00:45,289
We even know the ratio.

16
00:00:45,949 --> 00:00:49,929
The early universe left behind
a faint glow that still fills space.

17
00:00:50,709 --> 00:00:55,269
For every proton and neutron, there are
more than a billion particles of light.

18
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:59,120
Why the extra one? Nobody knows yet.

19
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:04,120
Our best theory of particles allows
a tiny imbalance, but nowhere near enough.

20
00:01:04,744 --> 00:01:06,824
Physicists are hunting for the rest.

21
00:01:07,684 --> 00:01:12,484
One suspect is the neutrino, the particle
that passes through almost everything.

22
00:01:12,983 --> 00:01:15,843
But even if they find the answer,
that would only tell us

23
00:01:16,073 --> 00:01:18,323
why there's matter instead of just light.

24
00:01:19,003 --> 00:01:21,123
Not why there's anything at all.

25
00:01:21,679 --> 00:01:24,219
Why is there a universe to make matter in?

26
00:01:24,599 --> 00:01:27,059
Why is there something,
instead of nothing?

27
00:01:29,469 --> 00:01:34,989
In 1714, the German philosopher
Gottfried Leibniz asked exactly that.

28
00:01:35,189 --> 00:01:38,689
And he added something
that sounds simple, but isn't.

29
00:01:39,145 --> 00:01:42,625
Nothing, he wrote,
is simpler and easier than something.

30
00:01:43,231 --> 00:01:46,251
At first glance,
nothing seems easier to explain.

31
00:01:46,991 --> 00:01:49,271
No rules, no beginning, no cause.

32
00:01:49,951 --> 00:01:52,351
With something,
you have to explain all three.

33
00:01:52,673 --> 00:01:55,773
So the strange thing
isn't that the universe is weird.

34
00:01:56,113 --> 00:01:58,553
It's that there's a universe at all.

35
00:01:59,150 --> 00:02:02,690
Physics has several ideas
about where the universe came from.

36
00:02:02,770 --> 00:02:05,650
But there's a problem
with every one of them.

37
00:02:05,930 --> 00:02:08,010
They all start with something.

38
00:02:10,510 --> 00:02:13,130
Let's start with the easiest version
of nothing.

39
00:02:13,710 --> 00:02:16,590
Take a box. Remove everything inside it.

40
00:02:17,093 --> 00:02:19,373
Every atom. Every speck of dust.

41
00:02:19,473 --> 00:02:21,553
Every particle of light.

42
00:02:21,633 --> 00:02:24,133
Cool it down as far as physics allows.

43
00:02:24,916 --> 00:02:27,316
That's nothing, surely. Empty space.

44
00:02:28,305 --> 00:02:33,145
It isn't. According to quantum physics,
empty space is full of fields,

45
00:02:33,225 --> 00:02:36,005
and those fields can never be
perfectly still.

46
00:02:36,574 --> 00:02:39,854
They keep trembling,
even in their lowest possible state.

47
00:02:40,554 --> 00:02:42,694
That lowest state is called the vacuum.

48
00:02:42,774 --> 00:02:44,854
And the vacuum is busy.

49
00:02:45,591 --> 00:02:47,271
You can even measure it.

50
00:02:47,851 --> 00:02:51,691
Put two metal plates in a vacuum,
far less than a hair's width apart,

51
00:02:51,771 --> 00:02:53,671
and they get pushed towards each other.

52
00:02:54,201 --> 00:02:55,781
Nothing touches them.

53
00:02:56,461 --> 00:03:00,001
In the usual picture,
the push comes from the vacuum itself.

54
00:03:00,701 --> 00:03:05,481
It's called the Casimir effect, after the
Dutch physicist who predicted it in 1948.

55
00:03:08,309 --> 00:03:10,689
And that gave some physicists an idea.

56
00:03:11,449 --> 00:03:15,649
If empty space is restless, maybe
a whole universe could come out of it.

57
00:03:16,173 --> 00:03:20,753
In 1973, the physicist Edward Tryon
asked whether the universe

58
00:03:20,983 --> 00:03:23,033
might be a fluctuation of the vacuum.

59
00:03:23,414 --> 00:03:25,654
But where would the energy come from?

60
00:03:26,262 --> 00:03:27,902
Maybe it doesn't have to.

61
00:03:28,442 --> 00:03:30,362
Matter carries positive energy.

62
00:03:31,082 --> 00:03:34,022
Gravity, in a sense,
carries negative energy.

63
00:03:34,622 --> 00:03:37,462
Add them up,
and the total might be exactly zero.

64
00:03:38,119 --> 00:03:42,979
In 2012, the physicist Lawrence Krauss
wrote a bestselling book on this idea.

65
00:03:43,799 --> 00:03:46,299
Its title was "A Universe from Nothing".

66
00:03:46,599 --> 00:03:49,859
And a philosopher of physics,
David Albert, wrote a review

67
00:03:50,089 --> 00:03:51,939
that went straight for the title.

68
00:03:52,477 --> 00:03:54,417
Quantum fields, he pointed out,
aren't nothing.

69
00:03:54,647 --> 00:03:56,177
They're stuff.

70
00:03:56,357 --> 00:03:58,297
Stuff that follows laws.

71
00:03:58,741 --> 00:04:00,941
That explains how the house was built.

72
00:04:01,361 --> 00:04:03,021
It doesn't explain the bricks.

73
00:04:05,301 --> 00:04:09,281
So take away even more.
No vacuum. No fields.

74
00:04:09,361 --> 00:04:11,541
No space at all, and not even time.

75
00:04:12,161 --> 00:04:15,681
In 1982, the physicist Alexander Vilenkin

76
00:04:15,761 --> 00:04:20,481
described a universe that appears
with no space and no time before it.

77
00:04:21,004 --> 00:04:22,944
In his model, a tiny universe appears

78
00:04:23,174 --> 00:04:25,864
through a quantum process
called tunnelling.

79
00:04:26,204 --> 00:04:27,884
Then it starts to expand.

80
00:04:28,675 --> 00:04:32,095
This is one of the closest things
physics has come to nothing.

81
00:04:32,497 --> 00:04:34,797
But the laws of physics are still there.

82
00:04:35,297 --> 00:04:37,337
They allow the universe to appear.

83
00:04:38,057 --> 00:04:41,857
And nobody has explained
why those laws exist in the first place.

84
00:04:42,538 --> 00:04:44,458
Each step took something away.

85
00:04:44,898 --> 00:04:46,598
None of them got to zero.

86
00:04:48,868 --> 00:04:52,048
Physics describes how one state
changes into another.

87
00:04:52,128 --> 00:04:55,908
But every explanation needs
something to start with.

88
00:04:56,433 --> 00:04:58,353
A state. A rule. Something.

89
00:04:58,813 --> 00:05:00,273
True nothing has none of these.

90
00:05:00,353 --> 00:05:02,653
And that's where physics
reaches its limit.

91
00:05:05,653 --> 00:05:10,253
In 1948, on BBC radio,
the philosopher Bertrand Russell

92
00:05:10,483 --> 00:05:13,373
debated this with a Jesuit priest,
Frederick Copleston.

93
00:05:14,730 --> 00:05:16,730
Copleston started
with the things around us.

94
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:19,210
Stars, planets, people.

95
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:21,730
None of them had to exist.

96
00:05:21,850 --> 00:05:23,610
Each could have been otherwise.

97
00:05:24,539 --> 00:05:26,479
Philosophers call that contingent.

98
00:05:27,319 --> 00:05:29,599
Something that exists,
but could have failed to.

99
00:05:30,246 --> 00:05:34,246
And Copleston argued that adding
up contingent things doesn't help.

100
00:05:34,846 --> 00:05:37,226
The whole universe is contingent too.

101
00:05:37,746 --> 00:05:39,626
It can't be the reason for itself.

102
00:05:40,158 --> 00:05:43,338
So, he concluded,
something must exist necessarily.

103
00:05:43,818 --> 00:05:48,198
Something that could not have failed to
exist, and needs no reason beyond itself.

104
00:05:49,058 --> 00:05:50,938
For Copleston, that was God.

105
00:05:51,607 --> 00:05:53,747
It's the answer Leibniz gave too.

106
00:05:54,347 --> 00:05:57,507
And notice what it does
to the question "what explains God?"

107
00:05:58,127 --> 00:06:00,727
For Copleston, that question
doesn't arise.

108
00:06:01,527 --> 00:06:04,087
A necessary being is where
the questions stop.

109
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:06,500
Russell wasn't persuaded.

110
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:09,840
Every person who exists has a mother,
he said.

111
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,840
That doesn't mean
the human race has a mother.

112
00:06:13,420 --> 00:06:15,860
Each thing in the universe
may need a cause.

113
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:19,420
That doesn't mean
the universe as a whole needs one.

114
00:06:19,682 --> 00:06:23,002
And he doubted that "necessary"
could apply to a thing at all.

115
00:06:23,522 --> 00:06:27,542
Only statements can be necessary,
he said. Not things.

116
00:06:28,294 --> 00:06:30,154
Russell's own view was simpler.

117
00:06:30,714 --> 00:06:33,674
The universe, he said,
is just there, and that's all.

118
00:06:34,859 --> 00:06:37,579
To Copleston, that was refusing to play.

119
00:06:38,039 --> 00:06:40,499
Years later, he wrote that if you won't

120
00:06:40,729 --> 00:06:43,619
even sit down at the chessboard,
you can't be checkmated.

121
00:06:44,258 --> 00:06:47,458
For Copleston, the chain of reasons
ended in God.

122
00:06:48,098 --> 00:06:51,218
For Russell, there was
no further explanation to give.

123
00:06:51,865 --> 00:06:54,265
And physics doesn't escape this either.

124
00:06:55,025 --> 00:06:58,085
If a law explains the universe,
what explains the law?

125
00:06:58,165 --> 00:07:02,585
Each of these chains ends somewhere.
The question is where.

126
00:07:05,185 --> 00:07:08,785
There's another way out,
and it turns Leibniz upside down.

127
00:07:09,420 --> 00:07:15,620
In 1996, the philosopher Peter van Inwagen
asked how many ways a world could be.

128
00:07:16,151 --> 00:07:18,951
There are endlessly many ways
for there to be something.

129
00:07:19,651 --> 00:07:21,891
One star, or two, or a billion.

130
00:07:22,531 --> 00:07:24,391
Different laws, different everything.

131
00:07:24,904 --> 00:07:27,544
But there's only one way
for there to be nothing.

132
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:29,900
His idea was simple.

133
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:33,140
If every way a world could be
were equally likely,

134
00:07:33,220 --> 00:07:35,680
the empty one
would practically never come up.

135
00:07:36,277 --> 00:07:39,417
Leibniz thought nothing
was the simplest state of all.

136
00:07:40,037 --> 00:07:43,217
Van Inwagen thought it was
far less likely than something.

137
00:07:43,799 --> 00:07:45,599
Not everyone is convinced.

138
00:07:46,379 --> 00:07:50,399
Why should every possibility be equally
likely, like tickets in a lottery?

139
00:07:51,219 --> 00:07:54,419
And endless possibilities
don't come with odds attached.

140
00:07:55,048 --> 00:07:58,248
And maybe the question itself
is stranger than it looks.

141
00:07:58,968 --> 00:08:00,368
Ask what nothing is like,

142
00:08:00,448 --> 00:08:02,968
and you're already treating it
as a kind of something.

143
00:08:05,768 --> 00:08:07,568
Physics can tell us a lot.

144
00:08:08,268 --> 00:08:11,808
One day it may even tell us
why there's matter instead of light,

145
00:08:11,908 --> 00:08:14,648
and how a universe might grow
from almost nothing.

146
00:08:15,210 --> 00:08:19,190
What it can't tell us is why
there's anything for its laws to be about.

147
00:08:19,550 --> 00:08:22,090
And philosophy hasn't settled it either.

148
00:08:22,590 --> 00:08:23,910
Every explanation we've heard

149
00:08:24,140 --> 00:08:27,090
either starts with something,
or stops at something.

150
00:08:27,730 --> 00:08:30,490
This is where explaining itself runs out.

151
00:08:33,300 --> 00:08:37,360
At least one small part of that universe
can now ask why it exists.

152
00:08:38,153 --> 00:08:40,633
Nothingness can't wonder
why there's something.

153
00:08:41,113 --> 00:08:42,553
Only something can.

154
00:08:43,822 --> 00:08:45,662
So here's a question for you.

155
00:08:46,382 --> 00:08:50,342
Is "the universe is just there" an honest
answer, or a way of not answering?

156
00:08:50,822 --> 00:08:52,172
Let me know in the comments.
